{
    "$comment": "Content addressing. A CID names a record by hashing it, so the same record has the same name everywhere and an altered record has a different name than the one anybody cited. Layered so that nothing is agreed out of band: multibase `b` (base32, lower case, unpadded), then CIDv1 (0x01), then the codec dag-cbor (0x71), then the multihash sha2-256 (0x12) with length 32 (0x20), then the digest. `cid` is the CID of `value` encoded as DAG-CBOR \u2014 so an implementation that fails these while passing encoding/dag-cbor.json has its hashing or its prefixes wrong rather than its encoder. A second set follows under `raw`. A record is DAG-CBOR and its CID reads `bafyrei\u2026`; a blob is bytes that are only themselves \u2014 an image, a model \u2014 and its CID uses the multicodec `raw` (0x55) and reads `bafkrei\u2026`. Both are CIDv1 sha2-256 and both are 59 characters, so the codec byte is the whole of what tells them apart. An implementation that hashes blob bytes and labels them dag-cbor produces a name of exactly the right shape that nothing else on the network agrees with, which is why this is pinned here rather than left to each implementation to get right.",
